## When to invoke

- Adding or changing CLI commands, subcommands, flags, or positional arguments.
- Argument parsing, help text, and shell-completion changes.
- Standard input / output handling, piping, redirection, TTY detection.
- Exit code conventions and error reporting to the terminal.
- Cross-platform behavior (Windows vs POSIX paths, line endings, signals).
- Packaging and distribution (entry points, bin scripts, installers, releases).

## Conventions

- Follow the project's existing command/subcommand layout. Don't introduce a new top-level command without alignment.
- Every flag needs help text. Default values, accepted ranges, and required-vs-optional status must be obvious from `--help`.
- Prefer subcommands over flag combinations once a command takes more than two or three orthogonal options.
- Long flags use `--kebab-case`. Short flags are single-letter and only added when frequency justifies the namespace cost.
- Read configuration from flags first, then env vars, then config files — in that precedence. Document the resolution order.
- Make output machine-readable when piped (`!isTTY` → no colors, no progress bars, structured output if a `--json` flag is supported).
- Use the project's existing logger. Stderr for diagnostics, stdout for the actual command output. Never mix them.

## Exit codes

- `0` only on full success.
- Non-zero for any failure; reserve specific codes (e.g. `2` for usage errors) only when documented.
- Don't `process.exit()` from deep call stacks — return errors up and exit from the top level.

## Output and UX

- Errors get short, actionable messages on stderr with a clear next step. No stack traces in normal user output (offer a `--debug` / `--verbose` flag for those).
- Long-running commands need progress indication on a TTY and silence (or structured progress) when piped.
- Respect `NO_COLOR` and `--no-color`. Don't emit ANSI escapes when stdout isn't a TTY.
- Prompts must not block when stdin isn't a TTY — fail with a clear error or take a flag instead.

## Security

- Treat all argument values as untrusted. Validate before passing to subprocesses, file APIs, or network calls.
- Never log secrets, tokens, or full config dumps by default. Redact sensitive values in `--verbose` output too.
- For commands that spawn subprocesses: pass arguments as arrays, never build a shell string. Refuse to run untrusted shell commands from config files.
- For commands that touch the filesystem: resolve and validate paths; reject `..` traversal outside the intended root.

## Out of scope

- Backend service code or HTTP handlers — defer to the backend agent if the CLI talks to a service in the same repo.
- UI components, browser behavior — defer to the frontend agent.
- Release pipelines / signing keys — propose first, don't change unilaterally.
